Since $n$ is prime, the only divisors of $n$ are itself and $1$. So $f(n) = 1 + n$. If $f(n)$ is prime then so too is $n$, as the only divisors of $f(n)$ are itself and $1$. By the same reasoning, if $f(f(n))$ is prime then $f(n)$ must be prime. Note that $f(f(2)) = f(3) = 4$, which is not prime, so $2$ is not a bouncy prime. If $n \ge 3$, $f(f(n))$ is odd. To be prime it cannot be a multiple of $3$, so we get the congruence $n + 1 \not \equiv 2 \pmod 3$, or $n \not \equiv 1 \pmod 3$. The smallest $n$ satisfying this congruence is $5$, $f(f(5)) = f(6) = 12$, which is not prime. The next prime is $7$: $f(f(7)) = f(8) = 15$, which is not prime. The next prime is $11$: $f(f(11)) = f(12) = 28$, which is not prime. The next prime is $13$: $f(f(13)) = f(14) = 24$, which is not prime. The next prime is $17$: $f(f(17)) = f(18) = 39$. The only divisors of $39$ are $1,3,13,39$, so $39$ is prime, and $17$ is a bouncy prime. $\blacksquare$
